---
import LedeReveal from './LedeReveal.tsx';
/**
 * ABOUT — slice R2.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "About":
 *
 *   Entrance      the drop cap. No kicker, no rule, no numeral, no
 *                 decorative mark, no visible title.
 *   Column window lede 1-9 · body 1-6 · closing held 10-12
 *   Image         NONE. Deliberately photo-free — the Cover portrait is
 *                 one scroll above it, and CONTEXT.md's "photography does
 *                 not sit in adjacent sections" rule is the reason.
 *   Rhythm        standard
 *   Margin holds  THE CLOSING LINE. CONTEXT.md's table originally read
 *                 "deliberately empty — the page's first breath" for this
 *                 row. That was written before the section could be seen.
 *                 What an empty margin produced here was a hollow right
 *                 half for the section's whole height, not a breath, and
 *                 a closing line with no edge to hang off. The table has
 *                 been updated rather than worked around.
 *   Length        full
 *
 * NO HEIGHT CONSTRAINT. No min-height, no svh floor. CONTEXT.md's R1.4
 * standing rule: the Cover is the ONLY section on this page with one,
 * and every other section is content-height. About is content-height.
 *
 * COPY is set verbatim from COPY.md Part 1 -> About. Four strings:
 * opening statement, two body paragraphs, closing line. Nothing is
 * reworded, nothing is added, nothing is cut.
 *
 * NO grid-column OR grid-row IS SET IN THIS FILE. Every placement is a
 * collapse-aware primitive from global.css — .win-about-lede,
 * .win-about-body, .win-about-close — and the behaviour of each is
 * written out in full above its implementation there, before the
 * implementation, in FOUR bands since R3 re-banded the sheet:
 * >=1280 / 1024-1279 / 751-1023 / <=750.
 *
 * R3 TOUCHED THIS FILE IN EXACTLY TWO PLACES, both below 1024 and
 * both consequences of the sheet re-banding rather than changes to
 * About's composition: the drop cap's two media-query bands are
 * deleted (N is now 3 at every width, so they are unnecessary), and
 * the comments are rewritten to four bands. Nothing at 1440, 1280,
 * 1279 or 1024 moved — verified by measurement, not by inspection.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH, so no treatment layer and no blend to protect; the
 * section carries no transform of any kind either way.
 *
 * GOLD. One saturated gold type accent is spent here: the drop cap.
 * CONTEXT.md's pinned budget names it explicitly as one of the three
 * ("the Legacy tagline, the About drop cap, the pull quote"), so this
 * is a budgeted use and not an addition. ZERO hairline-gold rule
 * contexts on this slice — there is no rule in this section at all.
 */
---

<section class="sheet about" id="sec-about">
  <!--
    About has no visible title, so the heading exists for the document
    outline and for screen-reader navigation only. .vh clips it with
    clip-path and keeps it in the accessibility tree; it is never
    display:none.

    The string is "About" because that is the `label` on the sec-about
    entry in src/data/sections.ts, and CONTEXT.md's locked heading
    structure requires the <h2> text and the folio label to be the same
    string. Verified against that file, not assumed.
  -->
  <h2 class="vh">About</h2>

  <!-- R18.1 — THE LEDE LINE MASK. The island nests INSIDE this
  paragraph; it does not replace it. The paragraph is the grid item
  and carries this section's placement, and Astro wraps a hydrated
  island in a real astro-island element — so an island mounted in
  the paragraph's place would become the grid item itself and move a
  left edge, which is the one thing this round may not do. Classes,
  placement and box are untouched. Only the text node moves into the
  island, and the island renders that same string verbatim until it
  has measured the rendered line boxes. Copy is COPY.md's. -->
  <p class="win-about-lede about-lede"><LedeReveal client:media="(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)" text={"Two decades of\u00a0seeing possibility in young people, and rebuilding the systems that serve\u00a0them."} /></p>

  <div class="win-about-body about-body">
    <p><span class="dropcap">D</span>r. Syreeta McClain is a high school principal, an educational consultant, and the <span class="keep">co-founder</span> of a foundation built to honor the people who show up when no one is watching.</p>
    <p>Today that work extends into college athletics, where she manages the careers of her three sons, bringing the same conviction to NIL, branding, and recruiting that she brought to school leadership.</p>
  </div>

  <!--
    THE LINE BREAK IS AUTHORED, not left to the wrap. Two sentences,
    two lines, at every width. Unforced, the break happened to fall
    between the sentences at some widths and inside "One through-line."
    at others — it set as one line at 1279 and at 390, and would break
    as "One / through-line." at widths between. A closing line whose
    shape changes with the viewport is not a composition.

    A <br> rather than two block spans: the paragraph's text content
    stays the single string "Different rooms. One through-line.",
    byte-identical to COPY.md, and nothing is added to the
    accessibility tree.
  -->
  <p class="win-about-close about-coda">Different rooms.<br />One through-line.</p>
</section>

<style>
  /* CONTEXT.md's per-section padding table gives About a flat 96, and
     the Cover above it 64. Sections MUST NOT share a padding token, so
     these values are declared here and nowhere else — there is
     deliberately no --vr-* token in this rule.

     THE TOP PADDING IS NOW ASYMMETRIC, and this is a deliberate
     departure from the table's flat "About 96". At a fixed 96 the lede
     was legible below the Cover at scroll 0, broken mid-phrase, and it
     read as a fourth item in the Cover's foot cluster rather than as
     the opening of a new section.

     The cause is that the two measurements are in different units. The
     Cover is 80svh, so the distance from the fold down to the lede
     scales with viewport height, while 96px does not. A fixed value
     cannot satisfy a viewport-relative requirement, so the padding
     becomes viewport-relative too, keeping 96px as a floor for short
     viewports where the fixed value was already sufficient.

     THE CONSTANT IS +12, NOT -40, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURED. The
     brief specified max(96px, calc(20svh - 40px)) from the model

         lede cap-top = (0.80 x vh) + 64 + padding-top

     but there is no +64. The Cover's 64px is `padding-block: 0 64px`
     INSIDE its own `min-height: 80svh` box, so it is consumed by the
     height floor rather than added to it. Measured at 1440x900: the
     Cover's bottom edge sits at exactly 720.0, which is 0.80 x 900,
     not 784. Built as specified, the lede's ink was still 28.0px above
     the fold at 800, 900, 1080 and 1440 tall, and 5.0px above it at
     700 — verified by scanning the actual pixels between the Cover's
     bottom edge and the fold, not by arithmetic.

     Two further corrections came out of that scan. The threshold is
     the lede's FIRST INK, which is 12px below its line box top — not
     its cap top, because Fraunces' lowercase ascender (the "d" in
     "decades") rises above the cap line. And the Cover is content-tall
     rather than 80svh on short viewports: at 700 it measures 583px,
     not 560.

     Corrected, with the brief's own 24px of margin preserved:

         first ink = coverBottom + padding-top + 12
         clears the fold when  padding-top > (0.20 x vh) - 12
         shipped:              padding-top = 20svh + 12

     Verified by pixel scan at 700, 800, 900, 1080 and 1440 tall: no
     lede ink anywhere in the band below the Cover, clearing by 24px at
     every height and by 47px at 700. What shows there is GROUND, not
     type, which is what the Cover's 80svh exists to create.

     THE svh TERM IS SCOPED TO 1024 AND UP, because the problem it
     solves only exists there. The Cover is 80svh only while its
     desktop composition is live; below 1024 it collapses to a stacked
     column with `min-height: auto` and becomes content-tall — 1131px
     at 390 wide, which already clears an 844px fold by 467px. Left
     unconditional it resolved to 180.8px at 390: 85px of extra dead
     scroll on a phone, buying separation that was already there.
     Below 1024 the padding is a flat 96px, matching the bottom, and
     the section is symmetric again.

     The desktop top stays asymmetric on purpose: it is buying
     separation from a viewport-height Cover, while the bottom is
     ordinary section rhythm against a content-height neighbour. */
  .about{
    padding-block: 96px;
  }

  /* R21 slice 1 SUPERSEDES THE svh TERM ABOVE. The Cover is now
     100svh, so the fold IS the Cover's bottom edge and the lede no
     longer needs buying below it: the band this padding created was
     20svh of empty ground under the portrait. Flat 96px, the value
     the bottom already carries. The note above is kept as history. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px){
    .about{ padding-top: 96px; }
  }

  /* ---- LEDE ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 1 of 3. Treatment copied from PillarPremier.astro's lede
     verbatim — same family, same four axes, same weight, same clamp,
     same line-height and letterfit — because CONTEXT.md's lede rule
     names that component as the reference every section matches.

     Obsidian, not Slate: it is the first type a reader meets after the
     Cover and it is the section's whole entrance apart from the cap.

     THE CLAMP IS NOT PillarPremier's. Its clamp(27px, 3.6vw, 52px)
     bottoms out at 27px, and this page's body tier is a FIXED 17px at
     every width — R1 carries no mobile step-down for --bio-size — so
     that floor lands the lede at 1.59x the body on a phone. CONTEXT.md
     records ~1.8x as the exact condition under which "the eye reads
     lede and body as the same tier", which is the failure this ratio
     rule was written against; 1.59x is inside it. The floor is raised
     to 52px so the ratio holds at 3x or better at EVERY width, the way
     R1 held the Cover's name-to-tagline ratio at 3.05x down to 360px
     rather than letting it fall to 2.6x. Measured: 3.22x at 1440,
     3.06x at 1280, 1279, 1024, 1023, 390 and 360, 3.76x at the 64px
     ceiling. Face, four axes, weight, line-height and letterfit are
     PillarPremier's, unchanged — only the size band moved. */
  .about-lede{
    margin: 0 0 clamp(44px, 4.4vw, 72px);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-lede);
    font-weight: 350;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);    /* was the token's clamp restated; reads the token so the lede cap reaches it */
    line-height: 1.18;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    /* balance, not pretty. `pretty` only protects the last line from
       being a short widow; it left "serve them." alone on line 3 at
       1440, well short of lines 1-2, because it optimises the rag from
       the bottom rather than evening the block. `balance` is the right
       tool for a 2-4 line block of display type and this is exactly its
       target case — the words and their order do not change, only where
       the browser breaks.

       PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT, not a requirement. An engine without
       `balance` falls back to normal wrapping and the lede is merely
       unbalanced, never broken. Chrome also declines to balance blocks
       over its own line-count threshold, which is why the phone widths
       below are unaffected and are reported as such rather than as a
       failure. The body keeps `pretty` — its paragraphs are far too
       long for balance to be either applied or wanted. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- BODY ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 3 of 3. Montserrat, the existing body tier, unchanged:
     --bio-size / --bio-lh are the tokens the old build's About used and
     the ones R1 carried forward in global.css with the note "the drop
     cap solve (R2) keys off these". The solve below does exactly that.

     PASS A — THE MEASURE IS CAPPED, AND IT IS A max-width, AGAINST
     CONTEXT.md's STANDING RULE. That rule reads "Measure is controlled
     by column span, never by max-width on the text element." It was
     overridden for About ONLY, by Jackson's ruling of September 16
     2026, because no span solves it: band 1 has no breakpoint above
     1280, so one span must hold 1280 through 1920, and
       6 columns   884px at 1920   98 characters  (the defect)
       5 columns   733px at 1920   82 characters  (still over)
       4 columns   368px at 1280   ~43 characters (under the floor)
     A span that fixed 1920 would need a new breakpoint, which the pass
     forbids. The column span still places the block; the cap only
     stops it growing past the ceiling. Nothing about the ancestor rule
     is touched: this is a leaf text block, and a max-width on it
     collapses no placement anywhere.

     670px IS MEASURED, NOT ESTIMATED. Swept 1px at a time from 560 to
     900 with the window held wider than every candidate:
       paragraph 1 holds <= 78 characters to 738px under the three-line
       cap and to 708px under the two-line cap
       paragraph 2 holds <= 78 characters to 670px; 671 renders 80
     so 670 is the widest measure at which BOTH paragraphs hold the
     ceiling. It is also the measure the 750px single-column threshold
     already ships (750 holds 78, 751 renders 80) — the same number
     reached from the other end of the page by a separate measurement.
     Paragraph 1 renders three lines at 670 under both caps.

     Written in em so it rides the body size: 670 / 17 = 39.4118em.
     It binds only where columns 1-6 are wider than 670px — above ~1500
     in band 1. 1440 and every narrower width render exactly as before.

     THIS IS COPY-DEPENDENT. The sweep measured today's two paragraphs.
     C1 owns copy and has not run; when it does, re-sweep, do not
     recalculate. */
  .about-body{
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--bio-size);
    line-height: var(--bio-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.004em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
    max-width: 39.4118em;
  }

  .about-body p{ margin: 0; }
  .about-body p + p{ margin-top: 1.5em; }

  /* ---- CODA ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 2 of 3. Fraunces at opsz 96 / SOFT 35 / wght 400 — all four
     axes stated, none left to Fraunces' defaults of wght 900 / WONK 1 /
     SOFT 100. These are NOT --fx-coda, which is opsz 48 / SOFT 45 /
     wght 360; the Phase B optical system is preserved untouched and
     this section states its own coordinates locally, the same way the
     Cover's `.legacy` does.

     SIZE. The brief puts the coda at roughly 1.5-2x the body and never
     near the lede. The body is a fixed 17px at every width, so that
     band is a fixed 25.5-34px — a clamp whose floor sits under 25.5px
     leaves the band on a phone no matter what its vw term does. The
     first pass ran clamp(21px, 2.1vw, 31px) and measured 1.26x at 1024
     and 1.24x at 390: outside the band at half the tested widths, and
     visibly so on the 1023 capture, where the closing line read as a
     caption rather than as a third tier. Floor and ceiling are now the
     band itself. Measured against the body: 1.86x at 1440, 1.66x at
     1280, 1.53x at 1024 and below. Against the lede: 1.73x at 1440 and
     2.00x at 390 — clearly subordinate at both ends.

     Obsidian rather than Slate, so the section closes on the same
     colour it opened on and the Slate body is framed between them. */
  /* ---- THE JOG, AND THE BASELINE SNAP ----------------------------
     THE CLOSING LINE SITS BESIDE THE BODY, IN THE SAME GRID ROW, AND
     IT IS ANCHORED TO THE BOTTOM OF THAT ROW, NOT THE TOP.

     Two passes got this wrong in two different ways. The first put it
     on its own row under paragraph 2. The second put it in the body's
     row but snapped its FIRST baseline to paragraph 1's LAST — which
     is level with the top third of the body block, so the closing line
     vacated the lower right entirely and left the corner hollow, the
     two columns ended on a ~100px staircase, and with nothing beneath
     it the block read as floating rather than as held.

     That placement came off the grayscale wireframe, whose boxes had
     roughly even mass. The real content does not: paragraph 2 is long,
     the closing line is three words, and the section is far taller
     than the wireframe implied. Same failure as R1.4 — a placement
     correct in wireframe and wrong against real content.

     SHIPPED: the closing line's LAST baseline sits on body paragraph
     2's LAST baseline. The section then closes on one level line, and
     the weight lands in the corner that was hollow.

     THE OFFSET IS COMPUTED, AND ANCHORING FROM THE ROW'S END MAKES IT
     MUCH SIMPLER THAN THE TOP-ANCHORED VERSION WAS. `align-self: end`
     puts the closing line's margin-box bottom on the row's bottom
     edge, and the row's bottom edge IS the body block's bottom edge,
     because the body is always the taller of the two items in the row
     (209px against 83px at 1440; the ratio never inverts, since the
     body is at minimum five lines and the closing is fixed at two).

     Measuring both blocks up from that shared bottom edge:

       row bottom -> P2's last baseline   = body line-height - B_body
       row bottom -> coda's last baseline = margin-bottom
                                            + coda line-height - B_coda

       so  margin-bottom = (body line-height - B_body)
                         - (coda line-height - B_coda)

     Both terms are a line-height minus a first-baseline offset — the
     distance from a baseline down to the bottom of its own line box.
     The offset is just the difference between the two faces' descender
     space. At 1440 that is 9.60 - 9.18 = 0.42px.

     --p1-lines IS GONE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The top-anchored
     version needed paragraph 1's rendered line count, which no CSS
     expression can produce, so it carried a hardcoded five-band
     lookup (>=1807 -> 2, 1292-1806 -> 3, 1280-1291 -> 4, 1122-1279 ->
     3, 1024-1121 -> 4) swept at 1px against this exact copy at this
     exact body size. Any copy edit or --bio-size change would have
     silently moved the snap by a whole 30.6px line. Anchoring from the
     row's end cancels the body's height out of the arithmetic
     entirely: no line count appears in it, so there is nothing to
     re-sweep and nothing to go stale. The bands are deleted, not
     documented. CONTEXT.md carries the warning against re-introducing
     that shape.

     B_body is the body baseline's distance from the top of its own
     line box: MEASURED at 21.000px with a zero-size inline-block
     probe, constant at every width because the body tier is a fixed
     17px/30.6px.

     B_coda is the same quantity for the closing line, and it cannot be
     a constant because the coda's size is fluid.

     PASS D — B_coda IS SOLVED AGAINST CHROME'S ROUNDED BASELINE, the
     model PASS A proved on the drop cap and on the body's own 21px:

         B = round(asc x F) + floor((lh - round(asc x F) - round(desc x F)) / 2)

     with Fraunces' 0.978 / 0.255. R2 wrote it smooth, as
     lh/2 + 0.36029em, and every residual it shipped is that
     difference and nothing else — predicted before measuring:
         1920  F 32.00  B 32  smooth 32.33   -0.33 measured -0.328
         1440  F 31.68  B 32  smooth 32.01   -0.01 measured -0.016
         1280  F 28.16  B 28  smooth 28.45   -0.45 measured -0.438
         1024  F 26.00  B 25  smooth 26.27   -1.27 measured -1.266
     so the "pre-existing -1.27px at 1024" was never a separate fault.
     Written with round() in the element's own em and lh, so a change
     to the coda's size re-solves it with no second measurement and no
     new custom property. The lede is in a different grid row and
     cannot reach this arithmetic: the residuals above are identical to
     the thousandth with the lede at 1-9 and at 1-11.

     Below 1024 none of this applies — the section is one stacked
     column, there is no body block beside the closing line and so no
     baseline to align to. It reverts to ordinary rhythm, unchanged
     from the accepted build. */
  .about-coda{
    align-self: end;
    margin: 0 0 calc(
              var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh) - var(--body-baseline)
              - (1lh - round(nearest, 0.978em, 1px)
                     - round(down, (1lh - round(nearest, 0.978em, 1px) - round(nearest, 0.255em, 1px)) / 2, 1px))
            );
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 35, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    /* NO text-wrap here. The break is authored in the markup above, so
       there is nothing left for `balance` to balance — it was inert
       and has been removed rather than left as decoration. The lede
       keeps `balance`, the body keeps `pretty`. */
  }

  /* ---- THE DROP CAP ----------------------------------------------
     THE ENTRANCE. The only section on the page that opens this way.

     CONTEXT.md's stated formula assumes a five-line span. FIVE IS
     WRONG HERE and was not used: body paragraph 1 renders three lines
     in columns 1-6 at 1440, and a cap taller than its own paragraph is
     the failure case the rule exists to prevent. N was measured from
     the rendered DOM at every breakpoint, capped at 3, and the cap was
     re-solved per breakpoint. Numbers are in the R2 report.

     THE SOLVE, per breakpoint:

       required ink cap-height  = (N - 1) x body line-height
                                  + body cap-height
       drop cap font-size  F    = required / capRatio(Fraunces)
       line-height         L    = 2 x b1 - 1
       margin-top               = (baselineOffset - capHeight_body)
                                  - F x (L - capRatio)

     where b1 is the glyph baseline's position inside the box at
     line-height 1, expressed as a fraction of the font size, and
     baselineOffset is the body baseline's distance from the top of its
     own line box. L = 2b1 - 1 is what puts the float's BOX BOTTOM
     exactly on its own ink baseline, which is what makes the float
     clear line N and not line N+1 — no trial-and-error line-height.

     capRatio, b1 and baselineOffset were MEASURED, not assumed:
     capRatio was read off rendered pixels at the exact four axes below,
     because the substitution from Bodoni Moda to Fraunces already
     forced one re-solve and the old 0.699 figure could not be carried
     over on trust. Montserrat's own cap ratio and baseline offset were
     measured the same way.

     GOLD — 1 of the 3 saturated type accents CONTEXT.md budgets by
     name. No other gold is introduced by this slice. */
  .dropcap{
    float: left;
    font-family: var(--display);
    /* opsz 144, SOFT 20, wght 300, WONK 0 — all four explicit.
       wght 300 is this prompt's value and it is NOT --fx-dropcap, which
       carries wght 380. The lighter stem is what keeps a 100px+ glyph
       from reading as a block of colour beside 17px body copy. */
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144, 'wght' 300, 'SOFT' 20, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: var(--gold);
    padding: 0;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;

    /* Solved values land here — see the media queries below. */
    font-size:   var(--cap-size);
    line-height: var(--cap-lh);
    margin: var(--cap-top) var(--cap-right) 0 0;
    margin-left: var(--cap-hang);
  }

  /* ---- THE SOLVED VALUES ------------------------------------------
     MEASURED CONSTANTS, read off rendered ink at 1000px, not assumed:

       Fraunces  opsz 144 / wght 300 / SOFT 20 / WONK 0
         cap-height ratio          0.70000
         baseline at line-height 1 0.86029 em   (= b1)
       Montserrat wght 300
         cap-height ratio          0.70000
       Body line box, as rendered
         font-size 17px · line-height 30.6px · baseline 21px from the
         top of its own line box

     'D' is the probe in both faces because it is flat-topped and
     flat-bottomed — its ink top IS the cap line and its ink bottom IS
     the baseline, with no round-letter overshoot to correct for.

     THE FIRST MEASUREMENT OF THIS WAS WRONG AND WAS CAUGHT BY THE
     RENDER, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE. The first probe
     clipped the screenshot to the glyph box's own edge; Porcelain page
     ground leaked into the outer rows of the scan and put Fraunces'
     cap ratio at 0.72200. The cap built on it landed its ink baseline
     2.1-2.9px SHORT of line N's baseline at every desktop width — a
     2.9% error, small on screen and fatal to the arithmetic. The probe
     now sits inside 60px of its own white padding with the clip taken
     strictly inside that, and the ratio is flat at 0.700 across six
     sizes from 1000px to 59px.

     Two independent cross-checks on the corrected numbers, because a
     measurement that has already been wrong once does not get taken on
     trust the second time:
       - b1 implies ascent - descent = 2 x b1 - 1. For Montserrat that
         gives 0.716 against a published hhea of 968/-251 = 0.717.
       - Fraunces is drawn on a 1000 upem with a 700 cap height, so
         0.700 is the value the outline actually carries.
     The old build's 0.69946 was near enough; it was still re-derived
     rather than trusted, which is what this slice was asked to do.

     LINE-HEIGHT is solved, not tuned: L = 2 x b1 - 1 = 0.72058 is the
     one value at which the float's BOX BOTTOM coincides with its own
     INK BASELINE. That is what makes the float release the text after
     exactly N lines — the box can then never reach into line N+1, and
     no trial-and-error line-height is involved at any breakpoint.

     ==== SUPERSEDED BY PASS A, September 16 2026 ====================
     Everything from here to the end of this comment is the R2/R3
     record, kept because its reasoning still stands. Its CONCLUSION
     does not: N is 2 now, not 3, and "N is 3 at every width" was false
     from 1807px up — see the PASS A block directly above `.about`.
     =================================================================

     N, MEASURED FROM THE RENDERED DOM AT EVERY BREAKPOINT, capped at
     3 per R2's brief. Re-measured in full after R3 re-banded the
     sheet, because N is a function of the MEASURE and R3 moved the
     measure at every width below 1024. Body paragraph 1 renders:

       band 1  1440  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
               1280  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
       band 2  1279  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
               1024  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
       band 3  1023  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px   <- was 2 lines
                950  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                901  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                900  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                850  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                800  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                768  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                751  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
       band 4   750  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                700  3 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                600  4 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                390  6 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px
                360  6 lines   N=3    cap 104.443px

     THE CAP NEVER EXTENDS PAST THE LAST LINE OF ITS OWN PARAGRAPH at
     any width — verified by scanning the rendered gold ink against the
     paragraph's own box, not by trusting the arithmetic.

     THE 872px BAND IS GONE, AND SO IS THE 1023px N=2 BAND. This is the
     part of R3 worth reading twice.

     R2 shipped three hardcoded cap bands: N=3 above 1024, N=2 from
     1023 down to 873, and N=3 again at 872 and below. The middle band
     existed for exactly one reason — below 1024 the sheet collapsed to
     one full-width column, the measure JUMPED from 513px to 943px, and
     paragraph 1 fell to two lines. The 872px boundary was a 5px sweep
     against that 943px measure, derived from nothing, and CONTEXT.md
     carries a standing warning that it goes stale silently: any edit to
     About's copy or to --bio-size / --bio-lh required re-sweeping it,
     and a wrong band does not degrade gracefully — it misses by a whole
     body line-height and the cap overhangs its own paragraph.

     R3 DELETED THE 943px MEASURE, so the two-line case it produced no
     longer exists anywhere. The widest measure below 1024 is now
     581.88px, at the top of the 8-column band, and paragraph 1 renders
     three lines there. Every narrower width renders three or more. N is
     therefore 3 at EVERY width on the page and the cap needs no band at
     all — the values in .about below are the only ones, and the two
     media-query overrides are deleted rather than left in place
     agreeing with the default by coincidence.

     This was not a goal of R3; it is a consequence of fixing the
     measure, and it is the second time a measurement has been made to
     cancel instead of being hardcoded (R2 did the same to the
     five-band --p1-lines lookup by re-anchoring the closing line from
     its grid row's END). CONTEXT.md's instruction to check whether a
     measurement can be cancelled before hardcoding a line count is the
     rule that retired both. THERE IS NOW NO SWEPT PIXEL BOUNDARY LEFT
     IN THIS COMPONENT.

     If a future pass widens the measure below 1024 past roughly 670px,
     the two-line case comes back and this comment is the warning. */

  /* ---- PASS A — THE CAP IS TWO LINES, SOLVED AGAINST ROUNDED METRICS
     THE REQUIREMENT IS TWO ALIGNMENTS AND NOTHING ELSE:
       1. the cap's cap-height top on line 1's cap-height
       2. the cap's baseline on line 2's baseline
     Two lines rather than three because paragraph 1 runs three lines
     or more at every width once the measure is capped (see BODY), so a
     two-line cap leaves line 3 onward running full measure UNDERNEATH
     the letter — which is what makes it a drop cap rather than a block
     pushed sideways.

     SIZE. Unchanged formula, N = 2:
         ink = (N - 1) x body line-height + body cap-height
             = 1 x 30.6 + 0.700 x 17 = 42.5px
         F   = 42.5 / 0.700           = 60.714px
     0.700 is closed: R2's pixel scan, R9.6's measureText() sweep, and
     the served woff2s (Fraunces 1400/2000, Montserrat 700/1000). The
     same formula gives 104.443px at N = 3, which is what shipped.
     Written from the tokens, so it cannot drift from the body tier.

     LINE-HEIGHT stays 0.72058 (= 2 x b1 - 1, the value that seats the
     float's box bottom on its own baseline, which is what releases
     line 3).

     MARGIN-TOP — THE ROUNDING RE-SOLVE, AND WHY THE SMOOTH FORMULA
     MISSED. The smooth solve gave 7.851px and landed at -1.01px (top)
     and -0.75px (baseline) at every width. The cause is Chrome's line
     layout, which does not use the face's metrics as fractions:
       - it rounds the ascent and the descent to whole pixels, each on
         its own, and
       - it FLOORS the half-leading to a whole pixel before adding it.
     So a line box's baseline sits at
         B = round(asc x F) + floor((lh - round(asc x F) - round(desc x F)) / 2)
     The body proves the model: Montserrat 968/-251 at 17px on 30.6px
     gives 16 + floor(5.3) = 21, the 21.000px --body-baseline has
     always measured. Fraunces 1956/-510 (0.978 / 0.255) at 60.714px
     on 0.72058 x 60.714 = 43.749px gives
         59 + floor((43.749 - 59 - 15) / 2) = 59 + floor(-15.125) = 43
     where the smooth formula assumed 43.749. That 0.749px IS the
     measured -0.75px. So the margin is solved against B, not against
     the box:
         margin-top = (line 2 baseline) - B_cap
                    = (--body-baseline + 30.6) - 43 = 8.6px
     Measured at 1920, 1440 and 1280: baseline 0.000px, cap-height top
     -0.256px — that remainder is antialiasing on the glyph's top edge,
     not position, and it is inside the 1.0px tolerance.

     THE ROUNDING IS WRITTEN IN CSS, not typed in as 8.6px, so a change
     to --bio-size or --bio-lh re-solves it with the same arithmetic
     Chrome uses. round() needs Chrome 125 / Safari 15.4 / Firefox 118.
     Firefox does not round font metrics the same way, so it may land
     up to about 1px off; it is still a two-line cap.

     NO BREAKPOINT, NO LOOKUP. One set of values at every width, which
     is the shape CONTEXT.md requires after deleting two lookups. */
  .about{
    /* Body baseline offset inside its own line box. MEASURED with a
       zero-size inline-block probe: 21.000px, and constant at every
       width because the body tier is a fixed 17px / 30.6px. */
    --body-baseline: 21px;

    /* N = 2 — PASS A. Derivation in the block above. */
    --cap-size:  calc((var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh) + 0.7 * var(--bio-size)) / 0.7);
    --cap-lh:    0.72058;
    --cap-asc:   round(nearest, 0.978 * var(--cap-size), 1px);
    --cap-desc:  round(nearest, 0.255 * var(--cap-size), 1px);
    --cap-base:  calc(var(--cap-asc) + round(down, (var(--cap-lh) * var(--cap-size) - var(--cap-asc) - var(--cap-desc)) / 2, 1px));
    --cap-top:   calc(var(--body-baseline) + var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh) - var(--cap-base));
    /* Space to the text it is set into. Fraunces' D already carries a
       right sidebearing; this is the optical remainder on top of it. */
    --cap-right: 0.045em;
    /* OPTICAL HANG, measured off the rendered face rather than
       estimated. Scanned at 2x from pixels: with no hang the D's INK
       lands at 67.50px against a 64px column-1 line, while the body
       paragraph's own ink lands at 64.00 — the cap's stem stood 3.5px
       right of every other left edge in the section, which at this size
       is plainly visible. 3.50 / 101.247 = 0.0346em is the D's own left
       sidebearing at these axes, so hanging by exactly that puts its
       ink on 64.00 with the body.

       An em, not a pixel: a left sidebearing is a fixed fraction of the
       em, so one measurement holds at the 60.722px cap in the collapsed
       band as well. Re-scanned at the shipped 104.443px to confirm.

       The lede's T measures 1.00px inside the same line and is left
       alone. One pixel on a 52px glyph is under the threshold at which
       it can be seen, and moving it would be a change with no visible
       cause. */
    --cap-hang:  -0.0346em;
  }

  /* ---- BELOW 1024 — bands 3 and 4 ---------------------------------
     THE CAP CARRIES NO OVERRIDE HERE ANY MORE. N = 3 at every width on
     the page after R3 re-banded the sheet; the two cap media queries
     R2 shipped are deleted, not neutralised. See the N table above.

     What remains in this block is the CLOSING LINE, and it is
     unchanged from the pre-R3 build. It covers the whole of band 3
     (751-1023) and band 4 (<= 750) in one rule, exactly as it covered
     everything below 1024 before, because .win-about-close spans the
     full width in BOTH of those bands — the closing line is
     deliberately NOT promoted into band 3's live margin.

     That is what keeps this rule correct without re-solving anything.
     There is no body block beside the closing line in either band, so
     there is no baseline to align to, and the computed snap gives way
     to ordinary rhythm. Both the alignment and the bottom margin the
     desktop rule sets are reset. Had the closing line been promoted
     into columns 7-8 at tablet width, this rule would have had to be
     split and the baseline-snap arithmetic re-solved for an 8-column
     band it was never derived for. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px){
    .about-coda{
      align-self: auto;
      margin-top: clamp(72px, 7.6vw, 118px);
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
  }
